---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { meta, rules } from '../lib/data';
import { amount, isoToLong } from '../lib/format';
import { site } from '../lib/site';

const withFactors = rules.filter((c) => c.classes.some((k) => k.forms.some((f) => f.elemental_factor)));
---

<Base title="How we compare prices" description="What we normalise, what we estimate, what we do not compare, and why no retailer can pay for position.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">How we compare prices</h1>
    <p>
      {site.name} answers one question: for a supplement you have already decided to buy, in the form you want, which
      product is cheapest per dose? We do not recommend what to take and we do not score quality.
    </p>

    <h2>What we normalise</h2>
    <p>
      Pack prices are not comparable: packs differ in size, servings differ in size, and labels quote amounts in
      different ways. For every product we work out the total amount of the ingredient in the pack and divide the price
      by it:
    </p>
    <ul class="sums">
      <li>servings = units in the pack × packs ÷ units per serving</li>
      <li>amount in the pack = servings × amount per serving</li>
      <li>price per standard dose = pack price ÷ amount in the pack × the standard dose</li>
      <li>cost per month = price per standard dose × 30</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every ranked row has a “Show the working” panel with these numbers and the exact words from the retailer’s listing
      that each one came from.
    </p>

    <h2>The standard dose is a comparison unit</h2>
    <p>
      Each ingredient has a fixed standard dose (for example {amount(100, 'mg')} of magnesium) so that every product is
      measured on the same scale. It is <strong>not a recommendation</strong> of how much to take. You can type your own
      daily amount into any table to see costs for that instead.
    </p>

    <h2>How listings are read</h2>
    <p>
      Retailer listings are read by software that includes an AI language model. The model’s only job is to copy facts
      out of the listing — form, amount per serving, pack size, serving size — and quote the words each fact came from.
      It never sees a price and never calculates anything. A number without a matching quote from the listing is thrown
      away. All arithmetic is done by ordinary, tested code using the rules on this page.
    </p>

    <h2 id="factors">What we estimate</h2>
    <p>
      Minerals are sold as compounds (magnesium <em>bisglycinate</em>, zinc <em>picolinate</em>). What matters for
      comparison is the amount of the mineral itself. If a label states it, we use it. If a label gives only the weight
      of the compound, we estimate the mineral content using the fraction below and mark the figure “estimated”.
      The fractions are calculated from each compound’s chemical formula and standard atomic weights. Real raw materials
      often contain water or added buffers, so an estimate can be higher than the true figure — a stated amount always
      wins.
    </p>
    {
      withFactors.map((c) => (
        <>
          <h3>{c.name}</h3>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Form</th><th scope="col" class="num">Fraction that is {c.name.toLowerCase()}</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {c.classes.flatMap((k) => k.forms).filter((f) => f.elemental_factor).map((f) => (
                  <tr class="offer plain"><td>{f.names[0]}</td><td class="num">{(f.elemental_factor! * 100).toFixed(1)}%</td></tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </>
      ))
    }

    <h2>What we do not compare</h2>
    <p>
      Products are ranked only against others of the same form. Magnesium citrate is never ranked against magnesium
      bisglycinate, creatine monohydrate never against creatine HCl, MK-7 never against MK-4. Products with several
      active ingredients are kept out of the main tables, because their price cannot fairly be assigned to one
      ingredient. Delivery charges are not included, because they depend on the size of your basket; each retailer’s
      delivery terms are listed below.
    </p>

    <h2 id="unverified">What “unverified” means</h2>
    <p>
      If a listing does not let us confirm the amount per serving, what that amount refers to, the pack size or the
      form, we do not guess. The product is listed separately with the reason and is never ranked. A common example:
      “Magnesium Citrate 200mg” could mean 200 mg of magnesium or 200 mg of magnesium citrate — a six-fold difference.
    </p>

    <h2>Testing claims and dietary labels</h2>
    <p>
      Badges such as “testing claimed on label” or “claimed: vegan” repeat what the retailer’s listing says. We have not
      verified them.
    </p>

    <h2>Prices and updates</h2>
    <p>
      Prices were last checked on {isoToLong(meta.generated_at)}. Each table and product page shows the date its prices
      were checked. The price shown is the retailer’s selling price on that date, including any sitewide promotion
      running at the time; voucher codes are not applied. Always confirm the price on the retailer’s site.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Retailer</th><th scope="col">Standard delivery</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {meta.retailers.map((r) => (
            <tr class="offer plain">
              <td>{r.name}</td>
              <td>
                {r.shipping.rule === 'free_over' && `Free over £${r.shipping.threshold_gbp}`}
                {r.shipping.rule === 'free_over' && r.shipping.flat_gbp ? `, otherwise £${r.shipping.flat_gbp.toFixed(2)}` : ''}
                {r.shipping.rule === 'flat' && `£${r.shipping.flat_gbp?.toFixed(2)}`}
                {r.shipping.rule === 'unknown' && 'See retailer'}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>No paid placement</h2>
    <p>
      We may earn a commission when you buy through our links. It never affects the order of a table: ranking is purely
      price per dose, and no retailer or brand can pay for position. See the <a href="/disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Report an error</h2>
    <p>
      Labels change and we make mistakes.
      {site.contact_email
        ? <>Every product has a “tell us” link, or email <a href={`mailto:${site.contact_email}`}>{site.contact_email}</a>.</>
        : <>A contact address will be published here before launch.</>}
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
